A||N||2||MR2KLR||Could My ECM be bad?||||1||
Z||000000||MR2KLR||12-09-2001||03:08 PM||||<BR>Check out this topic to see my previous posting.<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/015958.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/015958.html</A> <P>My gauges still do not work. I have been told to check the batery (I know it's good)  I replaced the belt (i'm now bypassing the AC compressor) i'm sure my Alt. is good because my car NEVER stals out ever and will crank and start perfect every time.<P>This is what i'm wored about. I unpluged the sensor in the water neck (4 cyl) I belive that that is the ECM's coolant sensor (am I right?) the cars idle instatly picked up (stoped mssing), the check engine light came on (obviously because I unpluged a sensor) and the fan came on. now even though the volt meter was in the red (i'd say 10-11 volts) the car ran fine great even. I went for a test drive 100ft up and down the street (twice) and it ran fine the whole time. my spedo does work. and my 6K tach thinks 3000rpm is 1000rpm it seems(the shift light seems to work properly). <P>Where does the Volt gauge get its reading from? I have also gotten a 12.00V reading from my volt meter at the battery. If my alternator was bad wouldn't my car not run. I think it's a bad ECM. what do you guys think.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <BR>Black 88 Coupe: 120 MPH spedo, Aux. gauge pod, 195/55VR14 Potenza RE-71 tires, GT exhaust & ground F/X Soon to have 16" White Beretta GTZ W/ 215/45ZR16's<BR><A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/TOMTOM81/MYNEWTOY/" TARGET=_blank>Tom's Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Nashco||12-09-2001||04:11 PM||||If you only have 12 volts at the battery while the car is running, you've got trouble.  Either your battery is toast, your alternator is toast, or both.  Lots of times one will go bad and if you keep driving it and putting off fixing it, the other goes bad in the meantime.<P>This is the first thing you need to fix if you're not getting proper voltage when it's running.  Remember, many of your electrical functions are basing things on a proper reference voltage.  If that reference voltage doesn't exist, the car will run like crap sometimes.  I replaced a bad alternator on a cavalier once that, along with a dead battery,  was causing the car to run exteremely rich and got several feet of the exhaust red hot!  It also threw codes for the ABS and made some of the dash instrumentation act very strange.<P>Get the voltage thing figured out...when it's running you should have at least 13.5 volts, usually a touch over 14.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||GTDude||12-11-2001||08:15 AM||||Can't be your ecm causing the trouble.  Gauges aren't controlled by ecm (except the speedo).  You definitely have an alternator/battery problem.  This MUST be repaired first.  Check voltage output at big post on the alternator, should be 13.5 to 14.5.  Also, check all your fuses.  A blown fuse can cause the alt. not to charge.  Fix the alt. prob. first and I can almost guarantee that most, if not all of your problems will go away.<P>GTDude<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
